WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:07.807
NARRATOR: Distributed systems operate across
multiple networked computers where components

2
00:00:07.807 --> 00:00:10.910
inevitably experience partial failure.

3
00:00:10.910 --> 00:00:18.803
NARRATOR: When a node slows down, it becomes
indistinguishable from a crashed server,

4
00:00:18.803 --> 00:00:24.875
forcing designers to make explicit assumptions
about timing and communication.

5
00:00:24.875 --> 00:00:33.607
NARRATOR: This uncertainty means we cannot
rely on simple timeouts to detect outages.

6
00:00:33.607 --> 00:00:42.471
NARRATOR: Instead, we must design protocols
that tolerate delayed messages, duplicated

7
00:00:42.471 --> 00:00:46.500
transmissions, and unpredictable network
schedules.

8
00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:54.357
NARRATOR: Understanding these constraints is
the first step toward building reliable

9
00:00:54.357 --> 00:01:01.500
consensus mechanisms that survive real-world
network chaos without assuming perfect

10
00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:05.071
synchronization or instantaneous failure
detection.

11
00:01:05.210 --> 00:01:11.841
NARRATOR: To guarantee consistency across
multiple nodes, we replicate state machines

12
00:01:11.841 --> 00:01:17.267
that process identical operations in the exact
same order.

13
00:01:17.267 --> 00:01:25.190
NARRATOR: Each server maintains a local copy
of the system state, but the critical

14
00:01:25.190 --> 00:01:31.981
challenge is ensuring they apply operations in
identical sequences despite network delays.

15
00:01:31.981 --> 00:01:39.064
NARRATOR: If servers apply commands
differently, the system diverges into

16
00:01:39.064 --> 00:01:40.481
conflicting states.

17
00:01:40.481 --> 00:01:48.794
NARRATOR: By forcing all nodes to execute the
same sequence of operations, we eliminate

18
00:01:48.794 --> 00:01:49.981
state divergence.

19
00:01:49.981 --> 00:01:56.747
NARRATOR: This approach transforms distributed
coordination into a single-threaded execution

20
00:01:56.747 --> 00:02:05.543
problem, where correctness depends entirely on
the order of applied operations rather than

21
00:02:05.543 --> 00:02:08.249
complex distributed state synchronization.

22
00:02:08.402 --> 00:02:15.415
NARRATOR: To enforce identical execution
order, we use a replicated log where every

23
00:02:15.415 --> 00:02:19.191
command is appended as a sequential entry.

24
00:02:19.191 --> 00:02:27.347
NARRATOR: Each log entry contains a unique
term number and a sequence index, creating a

25
00:02:27.347 --> 00:02:30.066
total order across all servers.

26
00:02:30.066 --> 00:02:37.392
NARRATOR: When a client submits a request, the
leader appends it to its log before

27
00:02:37.392 --> 00:02:38.369
acknowledging success.

28
00:02:38.369 --> 00:02:45.334
NARRATOR: Followers replicate the entry by
appending it to their own logs at the same

29
00:02:45.334 --> 00:02:45.798
index.

30
00:02:45.798 --> 00:02:52.736
NARRATOR: This log structure ensures that
every server records the exact same operations

31
00:02:52.736 --> 00:02:55.405
in the exact same order.

32
00:02:55.405 --> 00:03:04.311
NARRATOR: The log becomes the single source of
truth for system state, preventing conflicting

33
00:03:04.311 --> 00:03:10.673
histories and enabling reliable recovery after
crashes or network partitions.

34
00:03:10.826 --> 00:03:17.579
NARRATOR: A leader is elected to coordinate
log replication and client requests.

35
00:03:17.579 --> 00:03:25.030
NARRATOR: Servers start as followers and
transition to candidates when they miss

36
00:03:25.030 --> 00:03:25.651
heartbeats.

37
00:03:25.651 --> 00:03:34.347
NARRATOR: Randomized election timeouts prevent
split votes by staggering candidate timeouts.

38
00:03:34.347 --> 00:03:42.038
NARRATOR: When a candidate requests votes, it
increments a term number and includes its

39
00:03:42.038 --> 00:03:43.686
log's last entry.

40
00:03:43.686 --> 00:03:50.883
NARRATOR: Followers grant votes only if the
candidate's log is at least as up-to-date.

41
00:03:50.883 --> 00:03:58.133
NARRATOR: Once a candidate receives votes from
a majority, it becomes leader for that term.

42
00:03:58.133 --> 00:04:05.901
NARRATOR: Terms provide a logical clock that
prevents stale leaders from interfering with

43
00:04:05.901 --> 00:04:14.267
newer elections, ensuring only the most recent
leader can commit entries and maintain system

44
00:04:14.267 --> 00:04:14.865
progress.

45
00:04:15.002 --> 00:04:23.521
NARRATOR: Progress requires a majority quorum
of nodes to agree on log entries and

46
00:04:23.521 --> 00:04:24.130
leadership.

47
00:04:24.130 --> 00:04:32.755
NARRATOR: A majority ensures that any two
quorums share at least one node, preventing

48
00:04:32.755 --> 00:04:33.987
conflicting decisions.

49
00:04:33.987 --> 00:04:41.386
NARRATOR: If a network partition isolates a
minority, the majority partition continues

50
00:04:41.386 --> 00:04:44.469
operating while the minority stalls.

51
00:04:44.469 --> 00:04:51.434
NARRATOR: This design prioritizes safety over
availability during splits.

52
00:04:51.434 --> 00:04:57.420
NARRATOR: The quorum requirement guarantees
that committed entries are durable across

53
00:04:57.420 --> 00:05:02.862
restarts and that only one leader can exist
per term.

54
00:05:02.862 --> 00:05:11.172
NARRATOR: Without a majority, the system
cannot safely commit operations or elect a new

55
00:05:11.172 --> 00:05:17.702
leader, ensuring that conflicting histories
are never permitted during network failures.

56
00:05:17.858 --> 00:05:25.808
NARRATOR: The leader replicates log entries to
followers by sending AppendEntries RPCs.

57
00:05:25.808 --> 00:05:34.744
NARRATOR: Each RPC includes the leader's term,
the index of the entry to replicate, and the

58
00:05:34.744 --> 00:05:35.861
entry itself.

59
00:05:35.861 --> 00:05:41.611
NARRATOR: Followers respond with success or
failure based on log matching.

60
00:05:41.611 --> 00:05:49.429
NARRATOR: If a follower's log conflicts, the
leader backs off and retries with a lower

61
00:05:49.429 --> 00:05:49.950
index.

62
00:05:49.950 --> 00:05:56.843
NARRATOR: This process ensures all followers
eventually match the leader's log.

63
00:05:56.843 --> 00:06:04.648
NARRATOR: The log-matching property guarantees
that if two logs contain an entry with the

64
00:06:04.648 --> 00:06:09.665
same index and term, all preceding entries are
identical.

65
00:06:09.665 --> 00:06:17.211
NARRATOR: This property enables reliable
recovery and prevents divergent states during

66
00:06:17.211 --> 00:06:26.129
message loss or server restarts, maintaining a
consistent replicated log across the cluster.

67
00:06:26.282 --> 00:06:33.973
NARRATOR: An entry becomes committed when it
is replicated to a majority of servers and the

68
00:06:33.973 --> 00:06:36.857
leader's term matches the entry's term.

69
00:06:36.857 --> 00:06:43.821
NARRATOR: This rule ensures that only entries
from the current leader are committed,

70
00:06:43.821 --> 00:06:47.571
preventing stale leaders from overwriting
newer data.

71
00:06:47.571 --> 00:06:53.785
NARRATOR: Once committed, the entry is applied
to the state machine in order.

72
00:06:53.785 --> 00:07:01.435
NARRATOR: The commit rule relies on the
log-matching property to guarantee that all

73
00:07:01.435 --> 00:07:06.142
servers applying the entry share identical
preceding history.

74
00:07:06.142 --> 00:07:13.175
NARRATOR: This prevents conflicting state
updates and ensures that committed operations

75
00:07:13.175 --> 00:07:15.732
are durable across crashes.

76
00:07:15.732 --> 00:07:22.764
NARRATOR: The term-based commitment rule also
handles leader changes gracefully, ensuring

77
00:07:22.764 --> 00:07:28.517
that only the most recent leader's entries are
finalized.

78
00:07:28.658 --> 00:07:35.988
NARRATOR: Safety ensures that committed
entries are never lost or overwritten, while

79
00:07:35.988 --> 00:07:40.875
liveness guarantees that the system continues
making progress.

80
00:07:40.875 --> 00:07:49.015
NARRATOR: Raft prioritizes safety by refusing
to commit entries from stale leaders and

81
00:07:49.015 --> 00:07:50.893
requiring majority agreement.

82
00:07:50.893 --> 00:07:57.744
NARRATOR: Liveness is maintained through
randomized election timeouts and leader

83
00:07:57.744 --> 00:07:58.429
heartbeats.

84
00:07:58.429 --> 00:08:05.792
NARRATOR: When a leader fails, followers
detect the absence of heartbeats and trigger

85
00:08:05.792 --> 00:08:06.358
elections.

86
00:08:06.358 --> 00:08:14.750
NARRATOR: If a majority is available, a new
leader is elected and replication resumes.

87
00:08:14.750 --> 00:08:21.804
NARRATOR: If a minority is isolated, the
system stalls but remains safe.

88
00:08:21.804 --> 00:08:28.704
NARRATOR: This trade-off ensures that the
system never commits conflicting histories,

89
00:08:28.704 --> 00:08:36.858
even at the cost of temporary unavailability
during network partitions or leader failures.

90
00:08:37.010 --> 00:08:44.606
NARRATOR: Clients must retry operations when
they receive no response, but retries can

91
00:08:44.606 --> 00:08:49.281
cause duplicate commands if the original
request succeeded.

92
00:08:49.281 --> 00:08:56.549
NARRATOR: To handle this, clients include a
unique request ID with each operation.

93
00:08:56.549 --> 00:09:03.498
NARRATOR: The server checks if the ID has
already been processed and returns the cached

94
00:09:03.498 --> 00:09:04.888
result if so.

95
00:09:04.888 --> 00:09:11.835
NARRATOR: This idempotency mechanism prevents
duplicate state updates.

96
00:09:11.835 --> 00:09:18.988
NARRATOR: Retries are essential for
availability during network delays or leader

97
00:09:18.988 --> 00:09:19.638
elections.

98
00:09:19.638 --> 00:09:28.777
NARRATOR: By combining unique request IDs with
server-side deduplication, clients can safely

99
00:09:28.777 --> 00:09:32.585
retry without risking conflicting operations.

100
00:09:32.585 --> 00:09:40.181
NARRATOR: This approach ensures that clients
can recover from transient failures while

101
00:09:40.181 --> 00:09:45.245
maintaining system consistency and avoiding
duplicate state mutations.

102
00:09:45.410 --> 00:09:52.590
NARRATOR: Tracing a command through a
three-node cluster reveals how Raft

103
00:09:52.590 --> 00:09:53.895
coordinates consensus.

104
00:09:53.895 --> 00:10:00.938
NARRATOR: A client sends a request to the
leader, which appends it to its log and

105
00:10:00.938 --> 00:10:02.699
replicates it to followers.

106
00:10:02.699 --> 00:10:09.648
NARRATOR: Once a majority acknowledges the
entry, the leader commits it and applies it to

107
00:10:09.648 --> 00:10:11.038
its state machine.

108
00:10:11.038 --> 00:10:16.592
NARRATOR: Followers apply the entry in order
after replication.

109
00:10:16.592 --> 00:10:23.894
NARRATOR: If the leader fails, followers
detect the missing heartbeats and trigger

110
00:10:23.894 --> 00:10:24.502
elections.

111
00:10:24.502 --> 00:10:31.251
NARRATOR: A new leader is elected, and
replication resumes from the last committed

112
00:10:31.251 --> 00:10:31.770
entry.

113
00:10:31.770 --> 00:10:39.708
NARRATOR: This trace demonstrates how partial
failures, log replication, and majority

114
00:10:39.708 --> 00:10:44.038
quorums work together to maintain consistency.

115
00:10:44.038 --> 00:10:52.534
NARRATOR: The process ensures that every
command is processed exactly once, even during

116
00:10:52.534 --> 00:10:59.723
network delays or node crashes, providing a
reliable foundation for distributed

117
00:10:59.723 --> 00:11:00.377
applications.
